> But why? I mean what do we gain by closing the fds in the parent exactly? I really do not follow the logic of unsetting the env and closing the fds in the parent process. They could just stay open there as they are not used. The process exits directly after the child so it is not like it would hold them open for much longer?

If I run something like `systemd-socket-activate -l 4321 -a bin/podman run --rm -i fedora sh -c 'exec 3>&-; echo working; sleep 100'`

I expect that `nc localhost 4321` exits immediately, and doesn't wait for the container to terminate.

When we leak the fd into the parent podman process, that doesn't happen because the fd is kept alive by the parent podman.

This doesn't work today because conmon has the same leak, and we need the following patch there:

```diff
diff --git a/src/conmon.c b/src/conmon.c
index 6401df0..a4e2f0b 100644
--- a/src/conmon.c
+++ b/src/conmon.c
@@ -285,6 +285,16 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 		exit(127);
 	}

+	char *listenfds = getenv("LISTEN_FDS");
+	if (listenfds != NULL) {
+		errno = 0;
+		int i, lfds = strtol(listenfds, NULL, 10);
+		if (errno != 0 || lfds <= 0)
+			_pexitf("Invalid LISTEN_FDS %.10s", listenfds);
+		for (i = 0; i < lfds; i++)
+			close(i + 3);
+	}
+
 	if (logging_is_passthrough())
 		disconnect_std_streams();
```


Tell me if you prefer to not worry about this weird corner case.  Otherwise I can open a PR for conmon

> I expect that nc localhost 4321 exits immediately, and doesn't wait for the container to terminate.

Ack, thanks yes that was the missing point I did not consider, the container process should be able to close the fd before container exit.

But are we not holding the fd open in the podman run process (the child) still, not just conmon?

```
// Pass down the LISTEN_* environment (see #10443).
	if val := os.Getenv("LISTEN_FDS"); val != "" {
		if preserveFDs > 0 || len(ctr.config.PreserveFD) > 0 {
			logrus.Warnf("Ignoring LISTEN_FDS to preserve custom user-specified FDs")
		} else {
			fds, err := strconv.Atoi(val)
			if err != nil {
				return 0, fmt.Errorf("converting LISTEN_FDS=%s: %w", val, err)
			}
			preserveFDs = uint(fds)
		}
	}
```
This only passes the fds down never closes them, so this only works in the case of `podman run -d` not the foreground podman run like in your example?

